{
  "schema_version": "formalization-faithfulness-1",
  "role": "adjudicator",
  "task_id": "LEV-CH01-RIEMANN-INITIAL-VALUE-DEFINITION-PRODUCTION-20260908",
  "trigger": [
    "judge classifications differ",
    "direct judge requested adjudication",
    "round-trip judge requested adjudication",
    "round-trip judge classification is undetermined",
    "direct judge left dependency_coverage item D003 unclear",
    "direct judge left semantic_checklist item C11 unclear",
    "round-trip judge left semantic_checklist item C11 unclear",
    "round-trip judge left implication source_implies_translation unclear"
  ],
  "resolved_items": [
    {
      "item": "judge classifications differ",
      "resolution": "The appropriate classification is not-faithful-weaker. The formal predicate retains the initial-data requirements but does not retain the defining restriction to hyperbolic governing equations. Every source instance supplies the formal constraints under the explicit equation-satisfaction interpretation described below. The converse fails. Neither unrestricted quantification nor definitional validity constitutes genuine additional mathematical strength.",
      "primary_evidence": "Printed page 5, Section 1.2.1, defines the Riemann problem using a hyperbolic equation and special initial data. The elaborated target and D001–D003 contain only an unrestricted predicate applied to q and the two initial half-line equalities."
    },
    {
      "item": "direct judge requested adjudication",
      "resolution": "The equal-state issue cannot be conclusively settled from the supplied passage. However, either possible reading preserves source-to-Lean implication: source-admitted states satisfy the displayed branches whether or not the source additionally requires them to differ. The independent absence of hyperbolic-equation semantics establishes failure of Lean-to-source implication in either reading.",
      "primary_evidence": "Printed page 5 describes a single jump discontinuity immediately before (1.11), but (1.11) contains no inequality between q_l and q_r. D003 imposes the two branch equalities without a distinctness condition."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge requested adjudication",
      "resolution": "The reverse interpretation bridge can be supplied directly without adding an unsupported solution framework. For any governing hyperbolic equation in the source setting, let State be its real-vector state space and let evolutionEquation express satisfaction of that same equation in the source instance's chosen interpretation. Then the formal predicate is precisely the conjunction of that predicate with (1.11). This does not certify arbitrary formal predicates as hyperbolic equations.",
      "primary_evidence": "Printed page 1 introduces q with values in real m-dimensional space; printed page 5 combines the governing equation with initial data. D002 accepts any predicate on the same candidate field and conjoins it with D003 applied to the initial trace."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge classification is undetermined",
      "resolution": "The unresolved source convention concerning equal states remains reportable uncertainty, but it does not leave either decisive implication unclear. The reverse bridge establishes inclusion of source instances in the formal characterization; missing equation-class semantics prevents the opposite inclusion. The classification therefore need not remain undetermined.",
      "primary_evidence": "Both branches of source equation (1.11) appear verbatim in mathematical content in D003. Neither the target's binders nor D001–D003 require a hyperbolic equation. The equal-state ambiguity can only further restrict source instances, not invalidate their displayed branch conditions."
    },
    {
      "item": "direct judge left dependency_coverage item D003 unclear",
      "resolution": "D003's declaration meaning is completely determined: it requires exact equality to fixed states on the negative and positive real half-lines, leaves the origin unrestricted, and permits equal states. It matches the displayed formula exactly. Only its agreement with the prose's treatment of degenerate states remains uncertain; there is no unresolved Lean dependency meaning.",
      "primary_evidence": "D003 expands to (∀ x, x < 0 → data x = leftState) ∧ (∀ x, 0 < x → data x = rightState). Printed page 5, equation (1.11), gives those same strict branches."
    },
    {
      "item": "direct judge left semantic_checklist item C11 unclear",
      "resolution": "The interface boundary is correctly represented: neither explicit branch constrains q(0,0). Equal-state admissibility remains uncertain in the source. It is not appropriate to infer an inequality from the formula or explicit permission for the degenerate case from the absence of an inequality.",
      "primary_evidence": "The attached printed page 5 shows only x < 0 and x > 0 in (1.11), preceded by the jump-discontinuity description. D003 contains strict comparisons and no state inequality."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge left semantic_checklist item C11 unclear",
      "resolution": "The blind translation correctly identifies the free interface value and equal-state allowance of the formal data predicate. An independently supplied equation predicate could impose further conditions, but the initial-data constructor itself selects no origin value. No additional boundary defect is established; the same source ambiguity about equal states remains.",
      "primary_evidence": "Blind translation D002–D003 describes an unchanged equation predicate conjoined with the strict half-line conditions. The actual D002–D003 bodies confirm this interpretation, and source (1.11) supplies no origin branch."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge left implication source_implies_translation unclear",
      "resolution": "Resolve this implication as yes in the protocol's semantic comparison of defining constraints. Any candidate satisfying a source governing equation and (1.11) satisfies the translated conjunction when the equation predicate is instantiated with satisfaction of that equation. Distinct-state requirements, if intended, would only restrict those candidates further. This argument does not require the source to assert existence of a solution, and does not use the universal definitional identity as evidence of equation semantics.",
      "primary_evidence": "Section 1.2.1 defines equation-plus-data constraints. The blind translation and elaborated target both characterize evolutionEquation(q) together with precisely the two initial equalities; D001 and D002 introduce no other requirements."
    },
    {
      "item": "Dependency reuse and complete operator interpretation",
      "resolution": "D001 passes all arguments unchanged to D002; D002 combines the supplied predicate with the initial trace condition; D003 supplies the two branches. D004 requires all conjuncts, D005 uses exact state equality, and D006 expresses a defining equivalence. D007 and D010 select strict real order. D009 supplies both real coordinate domains. D008, D011, and D012 interpret every displayed zero as real zero. These effects preserve the displayed data semantics and introduce no hidden hyperbolicity, regularity, distinctness, or existence requirement. Reuse hashes support provenance only.",
      "primary_evidence": "The supplied complete direct and blind dossiers, dependency inventory, and reuse ledger agree on D001–D012. The explicit target shows each instance and zero argument. Source printed pages 1 and 5 supply the coordinate and initial-data conventions."
    },
    {
      "item": "Nonvacuity, object roles, and remaining semantic checks",
      "resolution": "The characterization is nonvacuous: with State = Real, leftState = 0, rightState = 1, the always-true predicate, and q(x,t) equal to 0 for x < 0 and 1 otherwise, its defined property holds. Intended hyperbolic instances are also compatible with the constructor, for example stationary two-state data for scalar zero-speed advection interpreted to allow discontinuities. Describing candidate solutions by constraints does not itself confuse problem data with an existence assertion. Currying, fixed states, time zero, interface normalization, and exact equality are preserved. Arbitrary State includes the intended real-vector spaces; that abstraction alone is not a decisive defect. No numerical algorithm, norm estimate, error bound, or asymptotic assertion is selected.",
      "primary_evidence": "D001–D003 permit the concrete witness and assert no existential conclusion. Printed page 1 discusses real scalar advection and real-vector states; printed pages 4–5 allow discontinuous solutions; printed pages 5–6 separate the definition from numerical applications and qualified solution behavior."
    }
  ],
  "implications": {
    "lean_implies_source": {
      "verdict": "no",
      "reasoning": "As a representation of the full selected definition, the formal property does not establish that its supplied governing object is a hyperbolic equation. D001–D003 allow an arbitrary predicate, including the always-true predicate with a nonconstant two-state initial trace. Such an instance supplies no identification or certification of the governing hyperbolic equation required by Section 1.2.1. The fact that one might separately find a hyperbolic equation compatible with a particular field does not establish that the supplied predicate represents that equation. Exact recovery of (1.11) therefore does not recover the complete source definition."
    },
    "source_implies_lean": {
      "verdict": "yes",
      "reasoning": "Interpret State as the source state space and evolutionEquation as satisfaction of the source instance's governing equation. A candidate satisfying the source equation and initial data satisfies evolutionEquation q and both universally quantified branches of D003, hence D002 and D001. This is a direct forgetting of the equation's hyperbolic qualification, not an assertion of solution existence. It remains valid whether the source permits or excludes equal states. The universally quantified unfolding theorem is independently definitionally valid; its validity is not being used to invent missing source semantics."
    }
  },
  "classification": "not-faithful-weaker",
  "accepted": false,
  "remaining_uncertainties": [
    "The supplied source passage does not conclusively determine whether equal left and right states count as a degenerate Riemann problem: the prose describes a jump discontinuity, while equation (1.11) supplies no explicit distinctness condition.",
    "The selected definition does not choose a precise solution framework or time interval. The formal constructor leaves those choices to its predicate; no particular choice is established by this audit."
  ],
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "category": "missing-hyperbolic-equation-semantics",
      "evidence": "Printed page 5, Section 1.2.1, expressly includes a hyperbolic equation. The target quantifies over every predicate on spacetime fields, and D001–D003 contain no structure, hypothesis, or interpretation tying that predicate to a hyperbolic equation.",
      "impact": "The declaration faithfully unfolds a generic equation-predicate and initial-data constructor, but does not faithfully characterize the full selected Riemann-problem definition."
    },
    {
      "severity": "note",
      "category": "degenerate-state-ambiguity",
      "evidence": "The prose preceding source (1.11) describes a jump discontinuity; its displayed branches contain no q_l ≠ q_r condition. D003 permits equal states.",
      "impact": "Full agreement on this boundary case remains uncertain. Either interpretation leaves the adjudicated implication directions and rejection unchanged."
    },
    {
      "severity": "note",
      "category": "preserved-initial-data-and-solution-roles",
      "evidence": "Source (1.11) and D003 agree on fixed states, strict half-lines, and initial time zero. D002 retains its equation predicate unchanged. The target asserts no existence, uniqueness, classical smoothness, or similarity property.",
      "impact": "The initial-data component and constraint-based presentation are supported. These correct components do not supply the missing governing-equation qualification."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "Independent inspection of the attached primary-source renderings and the supplied declaration evidence establishes an exact match for equation (1.11), but an omission from the surrounding definition: the governing equation must be hyperbolic. All twelve dependencies have been reassessed for their current effects; none restores that qualification. The reverse implication follows by explicitly interpreting the predicate as satisfaction of a source governing equation, without choosing an unsupported solution theory or claiming existence. The equal-state ambiguity remains unresolved but does not affect either implication. This is a weaker representation, not genuine stronger content or reduced applicability disguised as strength. Source and rendering hashes are supplied provenance and were not independently recomputed."
}
